==Grammar==
Structurally, the language is largely a [[relexification]] of English, although the [[semantics]] are closer to the native language of its users, [[Ibibio language|Ibibio]]. The definite article is ''dei'', and several prepositions alliterate or rhyme with their English equivalents: ''{{lang|dmf|su}}'' "to", ''{{lang|dmf|fra}}'' "from", ''{{lang|dmf|nai}}'' "by", ''{{lang|dmf|kin}}'' "in". Most words, however, resemble nothing in English or Ibibio, but appear to have been created without a specific underlying system. The [[morphology (linguistics)|morphology]] is not highly developed, but a few elements have been taken from English, such as the plural in -s (z?).

== Numbers and dates ==
The [[vigesimal]] numbering system and calendar reflect Ibibio norms. The calendar year contains sixteen four-week months.{{efn|The week is reported to consist of eight days, but it is not clear if it is 8 by Ibibio or English counting. What is 2 days from now in English is 3 days from now in Ibibio (with today [[Inclusive counting|counted inclusively]] as the first day), so starting a new week every 8 days when thinking in Ibibio means every 7 days when thinking in English.}}

